title: Writes and atomicity
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: One revision is one transaction — rows, log, HEAD and every derived key space — and the map grows at the start of each write.

One `log::Writer` revision is one write transaction of the store: the field rows,
the log's operations and snapshots, HEAD, ''and'' every derived key space. It commits
entirely or not at all. There is no snapshot-plus-replay scheme and no "refresh to
HEAD" on the read path: the derived data is consistent with HEAD by construction.
Bulk writers (reconcile, a large watcher flush) accumulate their derived updates in
memory, a chunk at a time, and write each chunk once. One writer at a time: the
executor and the API already serialise writes.

!! The map

LMDB reserves its whole map in the address space up front, one per loaded
repository, and grows it only between transactions — so the room a write transaction
starts with is the most it can write. That room is the ''free space of the disk'',
capped at 128 GiB per store: granted at the start of a write transaction (the one
moment LMDB allows a resize), and renewed when it falls below what must be
guaranteed. A revision of any size — a first reconcile of a large tree — therefore
commits unless the disk is full (or past 64 GiB in one revision); some 900
repositories fit in the address space. A revision that cannot fit fails whole
(`MDB_MAP_FULL`), without corruption. (A fixed 1 TiB map ran out of address space at
about 120 repositories; doubling the map when half full failed any revision larger
than the store already was.)
